{"kind":"report","text":"Checker review of the settle repair at head 9f6d1b86, for request c5f0cff9 and review request 0870eb69. Verdict: APPROVED. The head repairs a real race in a test helper, and its note says plainly that no run links that race to J1's failed gate. Only tests change. The cause of that one failure stays unproven, and this approval does not change that.\n\n## Your four questions\n\n1. **A forced window does not name J1's cause. It does name a cause. Land it anyway, and do not keep the request open waiting for a natural failure.** The forced run reproduces the identical assertion at the same point, and the repaired `settle` passes under the same window, so the race is real and the helper was wrong. Nothing ties it to that gate: 24 runs of the old `settle` under load passed, and the gate kept no outbox state. Waiting has no end, because a failure that has appeared once in many gates gives no date. The note's words are the right close. One addition would make the next failure answer the question: your limits say to read the outbox attempts if `intended` comes back, but nothing keeps them. Have a later head print the outbox attempts when that assertion fails, so that a failed gate retains them.\n2. **Yes, a delivery can still be in flight with both true, and the window is smaller.** `object.ts:897` runs `dispatch` from the alarm. If an alarm starts a pass after this round's call to that scope, and no commit moves any head before the second read, the round is quiet with a send on its way. A pass that a commit started is covered, because that commit moves a head. These stay outside the condition: a scope missing from the node list, and an attempt answered with `retry` or `busy`, which moves no head. I read this and did not reproduce it. It is no worse than the old `settle`. Add the alarm case to the note's limits, and let the helper's comment say \"no dispatch and no head moved\", not \"no send in flight\". `settle.test.ts`'s header makes the stronger claim.\n3. **The storage read: I see no change to what a test observes, beyond waking an evicted object a moment sooner.** `runInDurableObject` runs one synchronous `SELECT` in the object's turn and ends, and it holds nothing across an await. `dispatch` would wake that object anyway. What did change what tests observe is the longer wait, as your two partition tests show. I did not measure this. The full gate passes, so nothing that the suite states depends on the read.\n4. **The eight local loops: not in this head.** Each move can change what its test observes, as the two partition tests did, and none has failed. File one small item, so that the list does not live only in this note. `Graph.created` shares a file with the repaired helper, so take it first.\n\n## Other points\n\n- **The two partition tests.** They now accept `creating` or `active`, and `find` takes the first match. An old partition still marked `active` would match first. `directory-partitions.ts:147` sets the previous partition `sealed` in the entry that makes the successor, so at that point the old one should no longer match. Each test then asserts on the partition that it found. Both pass.\n- **The witness.** `settle.test.ts` forces the order with a gate and no wall-clock wait, and `control-d` turns it red by the named assertion when the old condition returns. That is the right shape for a race test.\n- **`platformNet.pause`.** It is a test seam in `packages/scope/test/worker.ts` and reaches no deployed path.\n- **Main has moved.** The dap mcp writer landed as 49c2226b after this head's checkpoint. `git merge-tree` of main and this head is clean, and the writer's change under these test directories is one file that this head does not touch. No gate has run on that merge.\n\n## Conclusions\n\n**Architecture.** Sound. Three copies of the loop become one, and `Dispatching` states what the helper needs.\n\n**Security.** Not applicable beyond this: no source under `packages/scope/src`, `packages/mcp/src`, `apps/room/lanes/src` or `apps/room/page` changes, so no Worker bundle changes.\n\n**Simplification.** Good, with the remaining eight loops as the debt that the note names. The added cost per round is unmeasured, as the note says.\n\n## What I checked\n\n- **Read.** The whole code diff, the note, and `directory-partitions.ts` and `object.ts` at the lines cited. I did not trace `delivery.ts`, and I did not read the forced-window patch.\n- **Evidence.** `SHA256SUMS` hashes to `b8974ff2…`, each listed file verifies, and no file is writable. The gate's head is `5ceada8b`, it exited 0, and its log shows 3467 plain and 936 native tests. From there to this head, only the note and its manifest change. I read `run-04` (exit 1, the named assertion), `run-05` (exit 0), `control-c` and `control-d` (each red by its named assertion), and `run-08` (24 identical passing runs). I did not rerun any of them.\n- **Tests I ran at this head.** `settle.test.ts`, `finding.scope.test.ts` and the two partition tests pass 5 of 5, once, with no added load. `check-notes` reports 0 warnings, `check-doc-links` 0 broken links, and `check-commit-messages` 0 flagged. The worktree stayed clean.\n- **Not done.** I used no independent reader, and I did not reproduce the alarm case above.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:538ca7610820034ba88d6a30c822670947b4bbf8","binding":"assigned","head":"9f6d1b862949336ea00dc9138cdd50d3af0ce124","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:a7a11e5490559c7ac3ec7c0960ad9bd0c9165ab0\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:dbd7c7ad92eef7153142ced7dba111689d2276b2\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:a44d619cc8432ff1d4d780221c42acb89dd26db9","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"approved"}}